# Class File Organization

- Place related types into feature-oriented folders (for example `Settings/Colors`).
- When adding new top-level types, create folders as needed to keep files grouped by responsibility.
- Avoid multi-class utility dumps; split unrelated top-level types into focused files.

## Private nested types (narrow exception)

Nest as **`private`** only when **all** of the following hold:

- consumed exclusively by one top-level class;
- a small record or POCO (data carrier only);
- **no methods, constructors with mapping logic, or computed properties**;
- **no attributes** (no `[Name]`, `[Format]`, `[TypeConverter]`, `[Xml*]`, etc.).

## When not to nest privately

- Type has serialization/mapping attributes or any behavior → separate top-level file (`public` or `internal`).
- Shared across multiple top-level types (even in the same assembly) → separate top-level file.

## Tests

Test classes stay one per file (unchanged).
